course: dictionary
lesson: 9600
title: Stricken: hit hard
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Stricken is an old past form of the verb strike, and it now works as an adjective. Picture a blow that knocks something down. A stricken town has been hit hard by a disaster, such as an earthquake.
explain: The word before «-stricken» tells you what did the hitting. In «panic-stricken», the weasels were struck by sudden fear. In «grief-stricken», the family was struck by deep sadness. The clue is always the first part of the word.
explain: «They were but four in all, but to the panic-stricken weasels the hall seemed full of monstrous animals.» The weasels are only four, but they feel afraid, so the hall looks huge and frightening to them. Stricken shows how strongly the fear has taken hold.
fact: Stricken means badly affected by something bad, such as illness, sadness or disaster.
fact: «A stricken ship» is a ship that has been badly damaged, perhaps in a storm.
fact: «Panic-stricken» means filled with sudden fear.
fact: Other compounds include «grief-stricken», «terror-stricken» and «poverty-stricken», and each one names the cause of the suffering.
fact: Stricken can also describe a face or look that shows great shock or pain, as in «a stricken look».
exercise: «The stricken town was quiet after the earthquake.» What does stricken mean here? | Very tall and grand | *Badly hit by a disaster | Newly painted and bright | Full of happy music
exercise: «The weasels were panic-stricken.» What does panic-stricken mean? | *Filled with sudden fear | Calm and sleepy | Very tidy | Showing great joy
exercise: In «a grief-stricken family», what caused the family's suffering? | Hunger | A football match | A new house | *Deep sadness
test: In «a stricken look», what does stricken show? | A cold wind | Sleepiness | Laughter | *Great shock or pain
test: A family with no money for food is best described as which? | *Poverty-stricken | Terror-stricken | Grief-stricken | Panic-stricken
exam: Why might an author write «panic-stricken» instead of just «scared»? | *It shows the fear was sudden and overwhelming, and names what caused it | It means the weasels were happy | It means exactly the same as scared, with no difference at all | It is a longer word and sounds more grand
